course: dictionary
lesson: 6424
title: Mutton: meat, and a saying
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mutton is the meat from an adult sheep. A young sheep gives lamb, which is milder. Mutton is older, so it has a stronger taste. «Picture a farm in 1100: the English-speaking farmer looks after a cow, a pig and a sheep, but when the meat reaches the French-speaking lord's table it is called beef, pork and mutton.» The animal is a sheep, but the meat has its own name.
explain: Mutton is an uncountable noun, so we say some mutton, not a mutton. Cooks use it in stews and curries. Mutton curry is common in South Asian cooking. Old recipes such as Irish stew and Scotch broth often use it, and in history it was a cheap everyday meat.
explain: The saying «mutton dressed as lamb» is informal and unkind. It describes an older person who wears clothes made for much younger people. Context helps here: if a story says Gran wears a sparkly crop top and skateboard trainers, the saying is about her clothes, not about a dinner.
fact: Mutton is the meat of an adult sheep.
fact: Lamb is the meat of a young sheep.
fact: «Mutton» comes from the French word «mouton», which means sheep.
fact: «Mutton dressed as lamb» is an unkind, informal saying about an older person in clothes for younger people.
fact: Mutton is used in stews, curries and old recipes like Irish stew.
exercise: «After 1066 English kept its animal words (cow, pig, sheep) but took French meat words: beef, pork and mutton.» Which name is the meat from a grown-up sheep? | *Mutton | Chicken | Pork | Lamb
exercise: Aunt Rose wears sparkly trainers and bright tiny tops at seventy. Her niece says she is «mutton dressed as lamb». What does that mean? | A sheep wearing a woolly coat | *An older person in clothes made for much younger people | A cook who burns the dinner | A very young child in a grown-up hat
exercise: Which meat is more likely to have a stronger taste? | *Mutton | Chicken | Both taste exactly the same | Lamb
test: Mutton is a noun. Is it countable or uncountable? | Countable, so we say a mutton | It is an adjective, not a noun | *Uncountable, so we say some mutton | It depends on the recipe
test: «Wild Dog smells roast mutton and goes to look.» Which word tells you the mutton is cooked? | goes | *roast | smells | look
exam: Which sentence uses mutton correctly? | Mum bought three muttons from the lamb shop. | Mum made a mutton of chicken and fish. | *Mum made a mutton stew with carrots and onions. | Mum said the mutton was a young sheep grazing in the field.
